[{:db/id 17592186231427 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0e005edd-92bb-566b-a438-96ee47079685" "question-357bba82-a114-59bb-9168-662c1a5b596f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231428 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e7130a2f-a2af-518c-8ac7-89bf1a4dc82f" "question-189994a2-e868-5656-b67a-5717b59ea689"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231429 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9d091e55-28a6-57e1-bec5-20aeba4179e3" "question-f5e9f5eb-87cf-56db-acd3-a7af3f0549dd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0e005edd-92bb-566b-a438-96ee47079685" :question/id #uuid "0e005edd-92bb-566b-a438-96ee47079685" :question/math-academy-id "q-64649" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+25y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=25\\,⟹\\,\\omega =5.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡5t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡5t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =5.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c1cb7679-3960-5f99-8d7f-3247916b22bb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c1cb7679-3960-5f99-8d7f-3247916b22bb" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1cb7679-3960-5f99-8d7f-3247916b22bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c47dc489-0d2d-5303-9b2d-fd259be71136" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed1b4b31-7de9-5f7b-bde1-a39eb1a63559" "answer-c47dc489-0d2d-5303-9b2d-fd259be71136" "answer-bc7b0520-863c-57f6-88ce-9c09e38df72d" "answer-53a865de-0fb4-5d42-8686-bc7acd219e51" "answer-3cf6a1db-f3c4-58d1-858e-32954c0da8e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed1b4b31-7de9-5f7b-bde1-a39eb1a63559" :answer/id #uuid "ed1b4b31-7de9-5f7b-bde1-a39eb1a63559" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c47dc489-0d2d-5303-9b2d-fd259be71136" :answer/id #uuid "c47dc489-0d2d-5303-9b2d-fd259be71136" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc7b0520-863c-57f6-88ce-9c09e38df72d" :answer/id #uuid "bc7b0520-863c-57f6-88ce-9c09e38df72d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53a865de-0fb4-5d42-8686-bc7acd219e51" :answer/id #uuid "53a865de-0fb4-5d42-8686-bc7acd219e51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=\\frac{25}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cf6a1db-f3c4-58d1-858e-32954c0da8e2" :answer/id #uuid "3cf6a1db-f3c4-58d1-858e-32954c0da8e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=\\frac{5}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-357bba82-a114-59bb-9168-662c1a5b596f" :question/id #uuid "357bba82-a114-59bb-9168-662c1a5b596f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64648" :question/problem "The position $y(t),$ measured in meters, of a particle oscillating with periodically forced harmonic motion without damping is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt-\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. Which value of the parameter $p$ causes the oscillator to resonate?" :question/worked-solution "The equation of forced harmonic motion without damping is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,\\omega =2.\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency $p$ of the external periodic force $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡pt-\\operatorname{sin}⁡pt$ coincides with the frequency of the complementary solution, which is $\\omega =2.$\n\nTherefore, the oscillator experiences resonance when $p=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c0c56303-137b-56d6-82e3-2da74edd11db"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c0c56303-137b-56d6-82e3-2da74edd11db" :answer-field/id #uuid "c0c56303-137b-56d6-82e3-2da74edd11db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-51dd82a0-1ae8-5048-89cd-03bffbf440aa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a2b9bcd0-d5ae-5c60-a5ca-71f2335c8fbc" "answer-51dd82a0-1ae8-5048-89cd-03bffbf440aa" "answer-9a0ad479-98f1-5fd5-bdf4-136d20ca6d95" "answer-bd0fc0c9-f5e2-5a02-9ef7-1c0fca86d2ca" "answer-533dac4c-3bed-5164-96dd-455ea3c1b7e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2b9bcd0-d5ae-5c60-a5ca-71f2335c8fbc" :answer/id #uuid "a2b9bcd0-d5ae-5c60-a5ca-71f2335c8fbc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51dd82a0-1ae8-5048-89cd-03bffbf440aa" :answer/id #uuid "51dd82a0-1ae8-5048-89cd-03bffbf440aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a0ad479-98f1-5fd5-bdf4-136d20ca6d95" :answer/id #uuid "9a0ad479-98f1-5fd5-bdf4-136d20ca6d95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd0fc0c9-f5e2-5a02-9ef7-1c0fca86d2ca" :answer/id #uuid "bd0fc0c9-f5e2-5a02-9ef7-1c0fca86d2ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-533dac4c-3bed-5164-96dd-455ea3c1b7e7" :answer/id #uuid "533dac4c-3bed-5164-96dd-455ea3c1b7e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p=\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e7130a2f-a2af-518c-8ac7-89bf1a4dc82f" :question/id #uuid "e7130a2f-a2af-518c-8ac7-89bf1a4dc82f" :question/math-academy-id "q-343673" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force $F(t)=-4sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}$ is applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky-4sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=-8sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=-8sin\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t),$ which is $\\frac{3}{2},$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-dff647f1-983f-5ba6-9928-87a2fdcf179e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-dff647f1-983f-5ba6-9928-87a2fdcf179e" :answer-field/id #uuid "dff647f1-983f-5ba6-9928-87a2fdcf179e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bebf495e-8b3b-5a9d-a588-759127f24c06" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bebf495e-8b3b-5a9d-a588-759127f24c06"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bebf495e-8b3b-5a9d-a588-759127f24c06" :answer/id #uuid "bebf495e-8b3b-5a9d-a588-759127f24c06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-189994a2-e868-5656-b67a-5717b59ea689" :question/id #uuid "189994a2-e868-5656-b67a-5717b59ea689" :question/math-academy-id "q-343698" :question/problem "A particle of mass $0.5\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. An external periodic force \n\n$$\nF(t)=2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)\\,\\text{N}\n$$\n\nis applied to the particle.\n\nThe position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with forced harmonic motion without damping and the system experiences resonance, what is the spring constant $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{kg/s}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of motion for a particle of mass $m$ moving with forced harmonic motion without damping under the action of a spring with spring constant $k$ and external force $F(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-ky+F(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$ and $F(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n0.5{y}^{″}=-ky+2{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t).\n$$\n\nSimplifying and rearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2ky=4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t).\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ here represents simple harmonic motion, and it can be written as\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants of integration, and $\\omega $ is the system's internal frequency.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n{\\omega }^{2}=2k\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe oscillator experiences resonance when the frequency of the external periodic force $f(t)=4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)$ is equal to the frequency of the complementary solution $\\omega .$ Since\n\n$$\nf(t)=4{cos}^{2}\\,(\\frac{3}{2}t)=2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)),\n$$\n\nthe frequency of the external periodic force is $3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{1}{2}{\\omega }^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{9}{2}\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-75523a18-6820-5b9b-bc7e-e9376c4fa924"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-75523a18-6820-5b9b-bc7e-e9376c4fa924" :answer-field/id #uuid "75523a18-6820-5b9b-bc7e-e9376c4fa924" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6c18230a-149d-58ba-93cf-40b239913f25" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6c18230a-149d-58ba-93cf-40b239913f25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c18230a-149d-58ba-93cf-40b239913f25" :answer/id #uuid "6c18230a-149d-58ba-93cf-40b239913f25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d091e55-28a6-57e1-bec5-20aeba4179e3" :question/id #uuid "9d091e55-28a6-57e1-bec5-20aeba4179e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-343770" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{1}{5}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{1}{5}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\frac{1}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $5.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =5.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =5$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(10D-25Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-(10C+25Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)]+25t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ 10D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-10C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{4}{5}$ and $D=\\frac{1}{5}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{1}{5}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{1}{5}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t+ϕ)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{1}{5}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\frac{4}{5}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\frac{1}{5}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and II match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c7ff387b-156b-5349-95c8-a5ca6f287ec2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c7ff387b-156b-5349-95c8-a5ca6f287ec2" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ff387b-156b-5349-95c8-a5ca6f287ec2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-99560652-fff1-54b3-ba9f-a43662eb6810" :answer-field/choices ["answer-99560652-fff1-54b3-ba9f-a43662eb6810" "answer-04294cfb-cb8e-5e85-95b0-be9065b6019e" "answer-23ab143c-f45b-5e8a-9480-58f173ceacfb" "answer-0155fefb-60d8-5dc5-9cb9-bfd9b44bc962" "answer-09521a1d-0037-59a4-b1e8-2b8b60a1d4de"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99560652-fff1-54b3-ba9f-a43662eb6810" :answer/id #uuid "99560652-fff1-54b3-ba9f-a43662eb6810" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04294cfb-cb8e-5e85-95b0-be9065b6019e" :answer/id #uuid "04294cfb-cb8e-5e85-95b0-be9065b6019e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23ab143c-f45b-5e8a-9480-58f173ceacfb" :answer/id #uuid "23ab143c-f45b-5e8a-9480-58f173ceacfb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0155fefb-60d8-5dc5-9cb9-bfd9b44bc962" :answer/id #uuid "0155fefb-60d8-5dc5-9cb9-bfd9b44bc962" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09521a1d-0037-59a4-b1e8-2b8b60a1d4de" :answer/id #uuid "09521a1d-0037-59a4-b1e8-2b8b60a1d4de" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f5e9f5eb-87cf-56db-acd3-a7af3f0549dd" :question/id #uuid "f5e9f5eb-87cf-56db-acd3-a7af3f0549dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-343755" :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $2.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =2.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =2$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[4(D-Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4(C+Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)]+4t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 4D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=\\frac{7}{4}$ and $D=0.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+ϕ)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{7}{4}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$\n\nOnly solutions I and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4c3d996d-7bb1-5375-bb04-132c705fc32a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4c3d996d-7bb1-5375-bb04-132c705fc32a" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c3d996d-7bb1-5375-bb04-132c705fc32a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4d110980-6336-5008-851d-def1533281f1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a5e9b00f-40af-5825-adee-8c54397db1e6" "answer-4d110980-6336-5008-851d-def1533281f1" "answer-db35a078-4935-5f77-a6a9-0351366c7e6c" "answer-10aed726-550b-5773-a3d2-e5a9aa903d37" "answer-de6388db-1542-562e-add4-ec8a83242db4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5e9b00f-40af-5825-adee-8c54397db1e6" :answer/id #uuid "a5e9b00f-40af-5825-adee-8c54397db1e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d110980-6336-5008-851d-def1533281f1" :answer/id #uuid "4d110980-6336-5008-851d-def1533281f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db35a078-4935-5f77-a6a9-0351366c7e6c" :answer/id #uuid "db35a078-4935-5f77-a6a9-0351366c7e6c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10aed726-550b-5773-a3d2-e5a9aa903d37" :answer/id #uuid "10aed726-550b-5773-a3d2-e5a9aa903d37" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de6388db-1542-562e-add4-ec8a83242db4" :answer/id #uuid "de6388db-1542-562e-add4-ec8a83242db4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317433 :question/problem "A particle is moving with forced harmonic motion without damping. The particle is acted on by an external periodic force\n$f(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t).$\nAssuming the system experiences resonance, which of the following could be used to represent the general solution for the position $y(t)?$\n\nI. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nII. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nIII. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nNote that $A,$ $B,$ $R,$ and $ϕ$ are constants." :question/worked-solution "Because the motion is undamped and the system experiences resonance, the forcing frequency equals the natural frequency $\\omega .$\n\nThe external force is $f(t)=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),$ which is sinusoidal with frequency $3.$ Therefore,\n$\\omega =3.$\n\nThe complementary solution for the associated simple harmonic motion equation can be written in three different forms:\n\n1\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n2\\. ${y}_{c}=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\omega t+ϕ)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)$\n\n3\\. ${y}_{c}=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\omega t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nAt resonance, a sinusoidal trial at frequency $\\omega $ duplicates the complementary solution, so we multiply by $t$ and take a resonant particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\n$$\n\nBy substituting the particular solution and $\\omega =3$ into the differential equation, ${y}^{″}+{\\omega }^{2}y=f(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[(6D-9Ct)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-(6C+9Dt)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)]+9t(C\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+D\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ 6D\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-6C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients, $C=0$ and $D=\\frac{5}{3}.$ \n\nSo, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(t)=\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nFinally, then, the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary and particular solutions. So, it can be written in any of the following forms:\n\n1\\. $y=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n2\\. $y=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t+ϕ)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\n3\\. $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\frac{5}{3}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)$\n\nSolutions I, II, and III match the forms above.\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
